50. Organisational Forms and Business Motives of the Firm
Paper: Industrial Economics (ECNDSE303) | Unit: II | Topic: Organisational form and alternative motives of the firm: types of organisation, choice, profit maximisation, sales maximisation, growth, value, managerial motives
Introduction
The organisational form determines ownership, control, finance and liability, while business motives determine how the firm behaves. Both are central to industrial economics.
Types of organisational form
• Private sector: sole proprietorship, partnership, joint stock company (private and public limited), cooperatives.
• Joint sector: joint ownership by government and private entrepreneurs (e.g., Gujarat Alkalies); combines public purpose with private efficiency.
• Public sector: departmental undertakings, statutory corporations, government companies (SAIL, ONGC).
Choice of organisational form
Factors: capital requirement, risk and liability (limited liability), control, taxation, flexibility, legal requirements, access to finance, and objectives. Small firms prefer proprietorship or partnership; large capital needs favour joint stock companies; strategic sectors and natural monopolies suit public ownership. Coase and Williamson: choose the form minimising transaction costs.
Alternative business motives
1. Profit maximisation (neoclassical): produce where MR = MC. Criticisms: separation of ownership and control, uncertainty, other goals, and satisficing behaviour (Simon).
2. Sales revenue maximisation (Baumol, 1959): managers prefer sales growth for salaries and prestige, subject to a minimum profit constraint. Output is higher and price lower than under profit maximisation; advertising is higher.
3. Growth maximisation (Marris, 1964): managers maximise the balanced growth rate of the firm subject to a security constraint (fear of takeover); trade-off between growth and valuation ratio.
4. Value maximisation: maximise the present value of future profits (shareholder wealth), the modern finance view.
5. Managerial motives (Williamson): managers maximise utility from salary, staff expenditure, managerial emoluments and discretionary profit.
6. Behavioural theory (Cyert and March): firms are coalitions, with satisficing, aspiration levels and organisational slack.
7. Limit pricing (Bain, Sylos-Labini): set price to deter entry.
Comparison
Profit maximisation fits competitive firms; managerial theories fit large corporations with separated ownership and control (Berle and Means); constraints from capital markets and takeovers push managers toward profit and value.
Conclusion
Organisational forms differ in risk, control and objectives; real firms balance several motives, and the corporate governance framework determines which prevails.